We think Whigs and the Hards have sustained themselves, though by desperate fight and close vote. St. Louis papers of the 10th do not give it up, but we think the Hards have it. The House will be about equally divided, but the Senate contains some fifteen or more majority of Loco Focos holding sway, and in some ably overruling in Joint Ballot, and Messrs. Benton and Atcheson. But a day or two may put a different face on affairs.

81. Lovrs has elected all Whigs but the Sheriff.
The following is the vote:

Whig, 501, Hards, 499.
Sheriff—........L. H. Allan, 133; J. C. Edwards, 127;
Greene—W. Raymond, 131; James Young, 129;
Congressman, 100; 800; 700; 450; 425;
James H. Lucas, 200; N. J. Lewis, 195;
Whites—2 to A. Sits, 185;
Wilson, 181; William Burn, 135.

All the Whig candidates elected but for Sheriff.
